{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# Inbound link removal never converges when a pair holds two relations (unlink is pair-scoped)\n\n## Context / Problem\n\nLinks are WRITTEN keyed on `(target_id, relation)`: `graph/_links.py::add_dependency` is\nidempotent per `(target_id, relation)`, so one ordered pair CAN hold two net-active\ndifferently-related deps (e.g. `blocks` + `relates_to`). But removal is pair-scoped:\n`rebar.unlink(id1, id2)` takes no relation argument and `_commands/unlink.py::_get_link_info`\nreturns \"the most recent net-active link for target\". Change 1276 (bug `2b16-9be0-a8f5-41d9`)\ntherefore added guard G5 in `rebar_reconciler/apply_inbound_records.py::_inbound_unlink_one`,\nwhich declines + logs when the pair's net-active relation does not match the relation whose\npeer link vanished.\n\n**How a remove record reaches G5 despite G4 (traced).** In a normal bidirectional pass,\nG4 (`inbound_differ.py::compute_inbound_mutations`, the action-aware same-pass link\nsuppression) drops an inbound remove whenever the outbound differ emitted a link mutation for\nthe same pair — and `outbound_links.py::_diff_links` usually re-emits an ADD for a\nstill-local, peer-absent dep, so in THAT case the pair \"converges\" by outbound re-adding the\npeer-deleted link and G5 is never reached. But the outbound ADD is deduped\n**direction-agnostically on `(vendor_type, target_key)`** and \"intentionally NOT deduped on\n`relation`\" (`_diff_links` docstring + `already_present` check), while the inbound removal\ndiffer compares in per-relation LOCAL vocabulary (`link_direction.py`: `blocks` and\n`depends_on` BOTH map to vendor type `Blocks`). So when a local dep's vendor type collides\nwith a remote link of the same vendor type in the OTHER direction — e.g. local dep\n`blocks → K` while the remote pair holds an inward `Blocks` link (which reads as `depends_on`\nin local vocabulary) — that remaining same-vendor-type remote link keeps the outbound ADD\ndeduped (`already_present` → skip), outbound emits nothing for the pair, `ob_entry`'s\n`link_add_keys`/`link_remove_keys` are empty, and the inbound remove record survives to G5.\nThe same holds for any pass where the outbound differ emits no link mutation for the pair\n(e.g. inbound-only application of records). In those G5-reached cases the decline IS\npermanent when the pair's most-recent net-active relation differs from the vanished one: the\nremoval record re-emits and re-declines on every subsequent pass, so inbound link removal\nnever converges for that double-related pair. This fix targets exactly that G5-reached path;\nthe G4-suppressed path (outbound re-add wins) is out of scope and unchanged. (Verified at\nruntime in a scratch store: one ordered pair holds `blocks` + `relates_to` net-active\nsimultaneously; `blocks` + `depends_on` on one ordered pair is rejected by the cycle guard;\npair-scoped `rebar.unlink` removes the most-recent link.)\n\n**Relation to 2b16's fail-safe ruling (removal-convergence is deliberately re-enabled).**\nThe operator ratified converting the G5 decline into a relation-scoped removal (see the\nratified-decision comment). The residual exposure — `managed_refs` marks a ref managed the\ninstant it is created locally, so a managed-but-never-pushed local link whose outbound ADD\nis permanently deduped by the direction-agnostic vendor-type check is state-indistinguishable\nfrom a genuine peer deletion — is PRE-EXISTING, not introduced here: today, when such a\nnever-pushed link is the pair's most-recent net-active link, G5's relation match PASSES and\nthe pair-scoped unlink already removes it. This change only makes the outcome\norder-independent (the exactly-named relation is removed instead of \"whichever is most\nrecent, else decline forever\"). It never removes a relation the record did not name, and a\nrecord naming a relation with no matching net-active local link removes nothing. The proper\ndiscriminator (persisted peer-link history distinguishing never-pushed from peer-deleted) is\na design pass of its own, filed as ticket `a4bd-4ca8-c44f-49bb` (linked `discovered_from`\nthis ticket) and deliberately out of scope here.\n\n## Approach (operator-ratified 2026-08-04 — see comment)\n\nMake removal symmetric with the write path, keyed on `(target_id, relation)`:\n\n1. **Graph-layer relation-scoped removal seam** — `graph/_links.py` gains\n   `remove_dependency(source_id, target_id, tracker_dir, relation)`, symmetric with\n   `add_dependency`: validates the relation, finds the most recent net-active LINK with\n   exactly that `(target_id, relation)`, writes the UNLINK event through the shared locked\n   write seam, and removes the reciprocal link for `relates_to`. The net-active replay in\n   `_commands/unlink.py::_get_link_info` gains an optional `relation=` filter (default `None`\n   keeps pair-scoped behavior) so the seam and the CLI stay in lockstep on one replay.\n2. **Reconciler convergence** — `_inbound_unlink_one` uses the relation-scoped removal, so\n   the G5 decline case converges by removing exactly the mirrored relation. The safety\n   invariant is preserved positively: when the named relation has NO net-active link locally,\n   the removal is a logged no-op and no other relation's link is ever removed.\n3. **CLI backward-compatible optional relation** — `rebar unlink <id1> <id2> [relation]`.\n   With a relation, removal is relation-scoped; with no relation, the existing pair-scoped\n   most-recent behavior is byte-for-byte UNCHANGED. `rebar.unlink` (library facade) gains the\n   same optional `relation=None` keyword.\n\nStrict TDD: RED behavioral tests first, then the implementation turns them GREEN.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/graph/_links.py` — add `remove_dependency` (relation-scoped removal seam) and an\n  UNLINK event writer mirroring `_write_link_event`.\n- `src/rebar/_commands/unlink.py` — optional relation on `_get_link_info`, `_write_unlink`,\n  `unlink_core`, `unlink_cli`; usage text updated.\n- `src/rebar/_lib_writes.py` — `rebar.unlink(id1, id2, relation=None, ...)`.\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/apply_inbound_records.py` — `_inbound_unlink_one`\n  performs relation-scoped removal instead of the permanent G5 decline.\n- `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/diffing/test_inbound_link_removal_2b16.py` — the G5 mismatch\n  test is revised to the new contract (still asserts the other relation's link survives and\n  a mismatching removal with no matching local link removes nothing); a new test proves the\n  double-related pair converges.\n- `tests/interfaces/lifecycle/test_unlink_relation_scoped_e39f.py` — new: two-relation pair\n  behaviors for the seam, the facade, and the CLI (including no-relation backward compat).\n- `src/rebar/_cli/help/unlink.txt` — the pinned `--help` text the CLI actually prints\n  (served by `_cli/_help.py::subcommand_help`) gains the optional relation.\n- `docs/cli-reference.md` — machine-generated from the help files under a CI drift gate;\n  regenerated with `python scripts/gen_cli_reference.py`.\n- `docs/user-guide.md` — the one-line `rebar unlink` description gains the optional relation.\n\nOut of scope (owned elsewhere): MCP `unlink_tickets` signature, binding_store.py, jira\nfetcher.py issuelinks merge, completion attestation/signing, show-command rendering.\n\n## Testing\n\nBehavioral, through public entry points:\n- Happy path: pair holds `blocks` + `relates_to`; relation-scoped removal of `blocks` leaves\n  `relates_to` net-active (and vice versa), asserted via `rebar.show_ticket` deps.\n- Reconciler: a double-related pair whose peer dropped one relation converges — the mirrored\n  relation's dep is gone after `_inbound_update_apply_links`, the other survives, and the\n  apply is counted (`applied == 1`); re-apply is an idempotent no-op.\n- Safety/negative: removal naming a relation with no net-active local link removes NOTHING\n  (G5's invariant, asserted positively) and is a logged no-op; `unlink` with an invalid\n  relation errors; `unlink` with no relation keeps the existing most-recent pair-scoped\n  behavior (existing tests stay green unmodified except the revised G5 mismatch test).\n- Edge: relation-scoped removal of `relates_to` also removes the reciprocal link.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] A test proves a pair CAN hold two differently-related net-active deps (checked: the new\n      lifecycle test creates `blocks` + `relates_to` on one pair and asserts both appear in\n      `show_ticket` deps).\n- [ ] A peer deletion of ONE relation in a two-relation pair reaches a defined end state: the\n      exactly-mirrored relation's link is removed and the reconcile converges (checked: new\n      2b16-file test drives `_inbound_update_apply_links` and asserts the named relation's dep\n      is gone, the other remains, and a second apply is a counted-zero no-op).\n- [ ] G5 never removes a link the peer still carries — asserted positively (checked: revised\n      mismatch test shows a removal naming a relation with no matching net-active local link\n      leaves the pair's other link intact and removes nothing).\n- [ ] `rebar unlink <id1> <id2>` with NO relation argument behaves exactly as before (checked:\n      all pre-existing unlink tests pass unmodified; a new test pins most-recent pair-scoped\n      removal).\n- [ ] Targeted tests, `make lint`, and `make typecheck` pass in the worktree venv (checked:\n      command output recorded in ticket comments).\n- [ ] Mutation-checked: re-introducing the pair-scoped call in `_inbound_unlink_one` turns the\n      convergence test RED; verdicts recorded on the ticket."}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785862052225475001,"uuid":"dc2cfda4-7b30-4908-9a8a-c6e0f38fd5bc"}